.\" Title: vrunner-thaw
.\" Author: vrunner contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrunner-thaw.1

.TH VRUNNER\-THAW 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunner\-thaw \- resume a frozen command via SIGCONT

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner
\fBthaw\fR \fIPID\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
Thaw (resume) a previously frozen command by sending it SIGCONT. The command resumes execution from exactly where it was suspended by \fBvrunner freeze\fR. All process state (memory, file descriptors, signal handlers) is preserved across the freeze/thaw cycle.

This is the CLI equivalent of \fBPOST /api/commands/:id/thaw\fR. The subcommand first discovers a running vrunner instance (using \fB\-\-target PID\fR if specified, or auto-selecting if only one exists), then resolves the given OS PID to a command UUID via the instance's API, and sends the thaw request.

After thawing, the command resumes normal execution and continues producing output in its VTTY. The web UI and CLI display update automatically as new output arrives.

.SH ARGUMENTS

.TP
.B PID
OS process ID of the frozen command to resume. This is the child process PID (shown in \fBvrunner list\fR output), not the vrunner instance PID.

.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
Thaw a frozen command:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner thaw 5678\fR
Command with PID 5678 thawed (SIGCONT) on instance 12345
.fi
.RE

.PP
Freeze and thaw cycle:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner freeze 5678\fR
Command with PID 5678 frozen (SIGSTOP) on instance 12345

\fB# ... do something else ... then resume ...\fR

\fB$ vrunner thaw 5678\fR
Command with PID 5678 thawed (SIGCONT) on instance 12345
.fi
.RE

.PP
Equivalent curl:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Resolve PID to command ID first, then thaw:\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:9090/api/commands/abc123/thaw" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{}'
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Command thawed successfully.

.TP
.B 1
Error (no running instances, PID not found, connection refused, etc.).

.SH SEE ALSO

.BR vrunner (1),
.BR vrunner\-freeze (1),
.BR vrunner\-stop\-command (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR vrunnerctrl (1)
